 ],
 "article": {
  "title": "Reskilling in the Age of Automation",
  "topic": "教育·职业技能重塑",
  "level": "CET-4",
  "words": 312,
  "paras": [
   "Automation and artificial intelligence are reshaping the workplace faster than many workers can adapt. A generation ago, a single skill could last a lifetime; today, the half-life of a technical skill is often measured in just a few years. This shift has turned \"reskilling\" from a niche concern into a mainstream one, discussed in boardrooms and community centres alike.",
   "The pressure, however, is uneven. Routine tasks are the first to be handed over to machines, and the people who perform them are usually those with the least room to retrain. At the same time, demand keeps rising for workers who can combine technical know-how with human judgement. Companies complain about labour shortages even as some employees feel invisible, as if the economy has moved on without them.",
   "Governments and employers are experimenting with answers. Some fund short, stackable certificates that let adults acquire new skills without quitting their jobs or taking on heavy debt. Others build partnerships with community colleges, running evening classes and offering paid study leave. The underlying idea is simple: learning should be continuous, not something that quietly ends with a diploma on the wall.",
   "Critics warn that micro-credentials can become little more than colourful badges unless employers genuinely value them. Access, too, remains unequal: those who most need retraining often have the least time and money to pursue it. Yet few disagree about the direction of travel. In a world where jobs change every few years, the ability — and the opportunity — to keep learning may matter more than any single qualification."
  ],
  "zh": "自动化与人工智能正以快于许多人适应的速度重塑职场：一项技能的“半衰期”已从“一辈子”缩短到几年，使再培训从边缘话题变为主流议题。压力分布并不均衡——重复性工作最先被机器接手，而做这些工作的人往往最没条件重新学习。各国政府和企业正在试验各种办法：资助短小、可叠加的证书，让成年人不必辞职或背债就能学到新技能；与社区学院合作开设晚间课程、提供带薪学习假，让学习变成持续的事。批评者提醒，若雇主不真正认可，微证书会沦为花哨的徽章；而且机会并不平等，最需要再培训的人常常最缺时间和金钱。但方向已少有争议：在工作几年一变的时代，持续学习的能力与机会，或许比任何单一的文凭都更重要。",
